msgctxt "help/238/0"
msgid "Roman Worship"
msgstr "Le Culte Romain"

msgctxt "help/238/1"
msgid ""
"The Romans were a superstitious people who spent a lot of time, effort, "
"and wealth trying to please the gods and influence them to act in the "
"desired manner. One common method for earning a god's favor was to "
"swear a vow to build that god a new temple, provided the god granted a "
"certain favor, such as victory in battle or in a political campaign.  "
"If the god came through, the Roman would be obliged to provide the "
"temple, as luxurious as he could afford.  Thanks to this, Rome was a "
"city with temples on almost every street corner.\n"
"\n"
"The Romans built temples to any number of things.  The established gods "
"had their worship places, of \n"
"#237Previous Page#/#239Next Page#\n"
"#235Help#/#236Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Les Romains étaient un peuple très superstitieux qui passaient beaucoup "
"de temps à tenter de plaire aux dieux et de les influencer dans leurs "
"actions selon leurs intérêts. La méthode la plus commune pour attirer "
"la faveur d'un dieu était de faire le voeu de lui ériger un nouveau "
"temple pour peu qu'il accorde certaines faveurs telles que la victoire "
"dans une bataille ou une campagne politique. Si le dieu exauçait les "
"souhaits du Romain, il était obligé de construire un temple aussi "
"luxueux qu'il le pouvait. Grâce à cela, Rome est une ville ou l'on "
"trouve un temple à chaque coin de rue.\n"
"Les Romains édifiaient des temples pour tout. Les dieux \"établis\" "
"avaient leurs lieux de culte, bien\n"
"#237Page Précédente#/#239Page Suivante#\n"
"#235Aide#/#236Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/239/0"
msgid "Roman Worship (cont.)"
msgstr "Le Culte Romain (suite)"

msgctxt "help/239/1"
msgid ""
"course, but there were also temples dedicated to foreign gods the "
"Romans adopted, to heroic legendary figures like Romulus and Aeneas, to "
"deified Emperors, to the city of Rome itself as a supernatural entity, "
"and even to nebulous ideals: one lovely and famous temple, the Ara "
"Pacis Augustae, or Altar ofAugustan Peace, is dedicated to the worship "
"of peace itself.\n"
"\n"
" Early Roman temples were small, round, colonnaded structures.  By "
"Imperial times, a more Greek look had taken over and temples became "
"rectangular, as well as much larger and more luxurious.  Three temples "
"of particular magnificence dominated Imperial Rome: the temple of Juno "
"Moneta, the temple of Jupiter Optimus Maximus, and the Pantheon.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#238Previous Page#/#240Next Page#\n"
"#235Help#/#236Tips#"
msgstr ""
"entendu, mais on trouvait aussi des temples voués aux dieux étrangers "
"adoptés par les Romains, aux figures légendaires telles que Romulus, "
"Remus et Enée, aux Empereurs déifiés, à la ville de Rome elle-même "
"tenue pour entité surnaturelle, et même aux vagues idéaux: un temple "
"charmant et fameux l'Ara Pacis Augustae, ou Autel de l'Auguste Paix, "
"est dédié au culte de la paix.\n"
"Les premiers temples Romains étaient petits, ronds et reposaient sur "
"des colonnes. A l'époque Impériale, la mode Grecque avait pris le "
"dessus et les temples sont devenus rectangulaires, plus grands et "
"luxueux. Trois temples magnifiques ont particulièrement dominé la Rome "
"Impériale: le temple de Juno Moneta, de Jupiter Optimus Maximus et le "
"Panthéon.\n"
"#238Page Précédente#/#240Page Suivante#\n"
"#235Aide#/#236Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/240/0"
msgid "Roman Worship (cont.)"
msgstr "Le Culte Romain (suite)"

msgctxt "help/240/1"
msgid ""
"The temple of the goddess Juno Moneta -- \"Juno Admonisher\" -- was "
"located on the north crest of the Capitoline hill. In addition to being "
"a center of worship to the dominant female goddess of the Romans, it "
"served as an Imperial mint and reserve.  (The name \"Moneta\" came down "
"to the present day as the word \"money.\")  On the south crest of the "
"same hill was the huge temple for Juno's husband (and brother) Jupiter "
"Optimus Maximus, first erected in 509 BC.  This temple burned down "
"three times in Rome's history and was rebuilt in larger and more "
"luxurious form each time.  Its final incarnation, created by the "
"Emperor Domitian, was entirely of white marble, with gilded roof tiles "
"and doors covered in solid gold plate.  Inside the temple could be "
"found piles of treasure heaped there as offerings by Roman generals who "
"had been victorious in war. \n"
"#239Previous Page#/#241Next Page#\n"
"#235Help#/#236Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Le temple de la déesse Juno Moneta -- \"Junon qui avertit\" -- était "
"situé sur la crête nord du Capitole. En plus d'être le centre du culte "
"de la déesse principale des Romains, il servait de monnaie impériale et "
"de réserve. (Le nom \"Moneta\" a donné naissance au mot \"monnaie\".) Sur "
"la crête sur de la même colline était le magnifique temple de l'époux "
"de Junon (son frère) Jupiter Optimus Maximus, érigé le premier en 509 "
"avant JC. Ce temple brûla trois fois dans l'histoire de Rome et fut "
"reconstruit, plus grand et plus luxueux à chaque fois. Sa version "
"finale, due à l'Empereur Domitien, était entièrement en marbre blanc, "
"couvert d'un toit de tuiles dorées et dont les portes étaient "
"recouvertes de plaques d'or massif.\n"
"#239Page Précédente#/#241Page Suivante#\n"
"#235Aide#/#236Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/241/0"
msgid "Roman Worship (cont.)"
msgstr "Le Culte Romain (suite)"

msgctxt "help/241/1"
msgid ""
"The most exceptional temple in Rome was the remarkable Pantheon, the "
"\"Temple of All Gods.\" It was first erected in 27 BC by the Roman "
"general Agrippa, but Emperor Hadrian so completely redesigned and "
"rebuilt it in 118 AD that archaeologists have been unable to decipher "
"Agrippa's original building plan.  The Pantheon's most stunning feature "
"is its huge dome with its perfectly round central skylight, made of "
"identical castings of the finest concrete.  The dome, 114 feet in "
"diameter, was the largest dome ever constructed until the twentieth "
"century. Thanks to its dedication to \"all gods\", it was able to make "
"the transition from pagan temple to church with the arrival of "
"Christianity, and it survives intact today, one of the most "
"awe-inspiring remnants of Roman architecture still in existence. \n"
"\n"
"#240Previous Page#/ #242 Next Page#\n"
"#235Help#/#236Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Le temple le plus exceptionnel de Rome était le remarquable Panthéon, "
"le \"Temple de tous les Dieux\". Il fut construit une première fois en 27 "
"avant JC par le Général Agrippa, mais l'Empereur Hadrien le redessina "
"complètement et le reconstruisit en l'an 118 de notre ère. Les "
"archéologues sont incapables de reconstituer le plan originel "
"d'Agrippa. L'aspect le plus étonnant du Panthéon est son merveilleux "
"dôme, sa lumière solaire centrale parfaitement circulaire, et ses "
"moulures identiques faites dans les matériaux les plus fins. Le dôme, "
"d'un diamètre de 43 mètres était le plus grand jamais construit "
"jusqu'au vingtième siècle. Grâce à sa vocation à \"tous les Dieux\", il "
"fit la transition entre les temples païens et les églises à l'avènement "
"du Christianisme, et il est demeuré intact jusqu'à nos jours.\n"
"#240Page Précédente#/#242 Page Suivante#\n"
"#235Aide#/#236Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/242/0"
msgid "Roman Worship"
msgstr "Le Culte Romain"

