msgctxt "help/490/0"
msgid "Theaters"
msgstr "Les Théâtres"

msgctxt "help/490/1"
msgid ""
"Theaters where plays, operas, and other shows are performed come in two "
"sizes: the basic Theater, and the Odeum. An Odeum will reach a larger "
"area than the basic Theater. Neither variety needs road access.\n"
"\n"
"Each will boost land values and, in a full game, will improve your "
"Culture Rating.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#491Tips# / #492History#"
msgstr ""
"Les théâtres, où des pièces, des opéras et autres spectacles étaient "
"donnés se présentent sous deux formes: le théâtre de base et l'Odéon. "
"L'Odéon draine un plus grand public qu'un théâtre de base. Aucune route "
"ne leur est nécessaire.\n"
"\n"
"Chacun entre eux augmente le coût foncier et, dans le jeu complet, "
"améliore l'indice culturel.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#491Trucs# / #492Histoire#"

msgctxt "help/491/0"
msgid "Tips for Entertainment"
msgstr "Trucs: les Théâtres"

msgctxt "help/491/1"
msgid ""
"When you have access to improved entertainment, you should consider "
"\"upgrading\" the ones you have already built.  You can easily replace "
"your old ones with odeums and coliseums, as they take up the same "
"space.  On the other hand, the Circus Maximus is larger than the "
"Circus, so you plan to clear extra space for it.\n"
"\n"
"If you can't afford to build the more expensive forms of entertainment, "
"you should still prepare space for them in your city.  For example, if "
"you try to arrange as many city blocks around a clearing that will "
"eventually hold a circus, than you won't need to build so many "
"circuses. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#490Help# / #492History#"
msgstr ""
"Lorsque vous avez accès à une meilleure catégorie de loisirs, vous "
"devez envisager d'améliorer ceux qui existent déjà. Vous pouvez "
"aisément remplacer les anciens par des Odéons et des Colisées qui "
"occupent le même espace. D'un autre point de vue, le Cirque Maxime est "
"plus grand que le Cirque, vous devez donc lui libérer de la place.\n"
"\n"
"Si vous ne pouvez pas vous permettre de construire les formes de "
"loisirs les plus coûteuses, vous pouvez leur préparer un espace dans la "
"cité. Par exemple, si vous essayez d'agencer autant d'îlots que "
"possible autour d'un espace qui pourrait contenir un cirque, vous "
"n'aurez pas à construire autant de cirques. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#490Aide# / #492Histoire#"

msgctxt "help/492/0"
msgid "Roman Theaters"
msgstr "Théâtres Romains"

msgctxt "help/492/1"
msgid ""
"The Maritime Theater at Hadrian's Villa\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#493Next Page#\n"
"#490Help#/#491Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Le Théâtre Maritime à la Villa d'Hadrien\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#493Page Suivante#\n"
"#490Aide#/#491Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/493/0"
msgid "Roman Theaters"
msgstr "Les théâtres"

msgctxt "help/493/1"
msgid ""
"Although theaters had long been wildly popular in Greece, they were "
"slower to catch on in Rome due to Senatorial resistance. Convinced that "
"the theater was an undignified pastime for a proper Roman, the Senate "
"decreed that all theaters had to be built as temporary structures and "
"were not allowed to have seating for the audience.  The first stone "
"theater was constructed by Pompey in 55 BC, who got around the "
"Senatorial proscription by placing a small shrine to Venus Victrix "
"above the seating area so that the theater could be considered a "
"temple.  \n"
"\n"
" With the dawn of the Imperial age, the prohibition against permanent "
"theaters was dropped. The \n"
"#492Previous Page#/#494Next Page#\n"
"#490Help#/#491Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Bien que les théâtres aient longtemps été fortement populaires en "
"Grèce, ils furent longs à s'implanter à Rome à cause d'une résistance "
"sénatoriale. Convaincus que le théâtre était un passe-temps indigne "
"d'un honnête Romain, le Sénat décréta que les théâtres devaient être "
"des structures temporaires ne permettant pas aux spectateurs de "
"s'asseoir. Le premier théâtre de pierre fût construit par Pompée en 55 "
"avant JC, qui transgressa les prescriptions sénatoriales en plaçant un "
"petit autel à Venus Victrix au-dessus des sièges de telle sorte que le "
"théâtre pouvait être considéré comme un temple.\n"
"A l'aube de l'ère impériale, l'interdiction des théâtres permanents fût "
"levée.\n"
"#492Page Précédente#/#494Page Suivante#\n"
"#490Aide#/#491Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/494/0"
msgid "Roman Theaters (cont.)"
msgstr "Les théâtres Romains (suite)"

msgctxt "help/494/1"
msgid ""
"Emperors had little concern for maintaining the dignity of the average "
"Roman, much preferring to have him amused and quiet.  By the height of "
"the Empire, Rome had three major public theaters which could hold "
"around 50,000 spectators each, plus many smaller theaters. In a "
"turnaround of opinion, theaters became the entertainment of the "
"dignified Roman, who looked down his long Roman nose at the rowdier "
"pleasures to be had in the circus and arena.  \n"
" The better plays performed in the Roman theaters were those by the "
"Greek playwrights; Roman writers generally produced plays with a lot of "
"fighting and melodrama, the ancient world's equivalent of the action "
"movie.  Some of these plays featured the brutal practice of actually "
"slaying a live victim  (always a helpless slave, of course) when an "
"on-stage \n"
"#493Previous Page#/#495Next Page#\n"
"#490Help#/#491Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Les Empereurs se sentaient peu concernés par le maintien de la dignité "
"du Romain moyen, préférant le voir amusé et calme. A l'apogée de "
"l'Empire, Rome possédait trois théâtres majeurs contenant environ 55000 "
"spectateurs chacun, ainsi que de nombreux théâtres plus petits. Après "
"un revirement d'opinion, le théâtre devint le loisir du dignitaire "
"Romain, qui contemplait au bout de son nez aquilin les plaisirs "
"tapageurs du cirque et de l'arène.\n"
"Les meilleures pièces jouées dans les théâtres Romains étaient celles "
"des auteurs Grecs, les écrivains Romains produisant généralement des "
"pièces parsemées de combats. Certaines de ces pièces mettaient en scène "
"la pratique brutale qui consistait à tuer réellement une victime "
"vivante (toujours un esclave, bien sûr) quand la mise en scène "
"nécessitait un cadavre\n"
"#493Page Précédente#/#495Page Suivante#\n"
"#490Aide#/#491Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/495/0"
msgid "Roman Theaters (cont.)"
msgstr "Les théâtres Romains (suite)"

msgctxt "help/495/1"
msgid ""
"death was called for. The formal theater used only male actors, but "
"both men and women performed in less formal presentations, and Rome had "
"several famous actresses with hordes of male admirers.  \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#494Previous Page#\n"
"#490Help#/#491Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Seuls les hommes pouvaient jouer dans les pièces classiques, mais "
"hommes et femmes se produisaient dans des spectacles moins guindés, et "
"Rome connut plusieurs actrices fameuses qui avaient des hordes "
"d'admirateurs.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
