msgctxt "help/523/0"
msgid "The Roman Arena"
msgstr "Les Arènes"

msgctxt "help/523/1"
msgid ""
"Unlike the theater and the circus, which were designed and used for "
"specific activities, the Arena was an all-purpose sporting stadium "
"where a wide variety of events, usually violent and bloody, were staged "
"for the public's amusement.  The arena was used for gladiatorial "
"combats, wild animal fights, war games, and atheletic competitions. It "
"could even be filled with water and used to stage small-scale naval "
"battles.  \n"
"\n"
" The most famous arena in Rome is, of course, the Colosseum.  This huge "
"building was constructed at the end of the forum valley, between the "
"Palatine and Esquiline hills, on a site where Nero had put an \n"
"#522Previous Page#/#524 Next Page#\n"
"#520Help#/#521Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Contrairement au théâtre et au cirque, conçus et utilisés pour des "
"activités spécifiques, l'arène était un stade omnisports dans lequel on "
"voyait toutes sortes de spectacles, généralement violents et sanglants, "
"mis en scène pour divertir le public. On les utilisait pour les combats "
"de gladiateurs, d'animaux sauvages, pour les jeux guerriers, et les "
"compétitions athlétiques. On pouvait même les remplir d'eau pour des "
"combats navals.\n"
"\n"
"L'arène la plus célèbre de Rome était, bien sûr, le Colisée. Cet "
"édifice énorme était construit à l'extrémité de la vallée du Forum, "
"entre les collines du Palatin et de l'Esquilin,\n"
"#522Page Précédente#/#524 Page Suivante#\n"
"#520Aide#/#521Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/524/0"
msgid "The Roman Arena (cont.)"
msgstr "Les Arènes Romaines (suite)"

msgctxt "help/524/1"
msgid ""
"artifical lake to go with his much-hated Golden Palace.  As a sign of "
"conciliation with the public, Nero's eventual successor Vespasian (who "
"took the Imperium after a series of bloody civil wars) drained the lake "
"and commissioned the building of the Colosseum for the explicit purpose "
"of providing free games for the public.  He died before it was "
"completed, however, and it was his son and sucessor Titus who "
"inaugerated the building in 80 AD.  \n"
"\n"
" The Colosseum, more properly known as the Flavian Amphitheater, had "
"three stories of seating plus an \"attic\" level.  It measured a third of "
"a mile around, and could seat 45,000, with room for another 5,000 "
"standing spectators.  It also featured a canvas awning, manned by "
"experienced sailors, which could be extended to give the spectators "
"some protection from \n"
"#523Previous Page#/#525 Next Page#\n"
"#520Help#/#521Tips#"
msgstr ""
"sur un site où Néron avait construit un lac artificiel en harmonie avec "
"son palais doré si détesté. En signe de conciliation avec le public, le "
"successeur de Néron, Vespasien (qui prît le pouvoir après une série de "
"sanglantes guerres civiles), assécha le lac et commanda la construction "
"du Colisée dont la vocation serait d'offrir des jeux gratuits au "
"public. Il mourut avant la fin des travaux. Ce fut son fils et "
"successeur, Titus, qui inaugura l'édifice en 80 après JC.\n"
"\n"
"Le Colisée, connu sous le nom d'Amphithéâtre de Flavien possédait trois "
"étages de sièges, plus un niveau \"mansarde\". Son diamètre était de 500 "
"mètres, il avait 45 000 places assises ainsi que 5000 places debout. il "
"était équipé d'un auvent en forme de voile manié par des marins "
"expérimentés,\n"
"#523Page Précédente#/#525 Page Suivante#\n"
"#520Aide#/#521Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/525/0"
msgid "The Roman Arena (cont.)"
msgstr "Les Arènes Romaines (suite)"

msgctxt "help/525/1"
msgid ""
"sun and rain.  \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
