msgctxt "help/580/0"
msgid "Grammaticus"
msgstr "Grammaire"

msgctxt "help/580/1"
msgid ""
"The Grammaticus is a school for young children (older children attend "
"the Rhetor). Housing needs schools to develop -- a lack of schools acts "
"as a Limiting Factor on development.\n"
"\n"
"A Grammaticus and a Rhetor will serve a surrounding radius, so each "
"pocket of housing may need its own schools.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#581Tips# / #582History#"
msgstr ""
"L'école de Grammaire est une école pour les jeunes enfants (les plus "
"grands fréquentent l'école de rhétorique). L'Habitat a besoin du "
"développement de l'école -- le manque d'écoles est facteur de "
"limitation du développement.\n"
"\n"
"Les écoles de Grammaire et de Rhétorique desservent leur voisinage "
"aussi, il faut des écoles dans chaque groupe d'habitations.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#581Trucs# / #582Histoire#"

msgctxt "help/581/0"
msgid "Tips for Education"
msgstr "Trucs: l'Education"

msgctxt "help/581/1"
msgid ""
"Your city won't be able to produce the best housing until you can build "
"a library.  Make sure you build a large enough population so that the "
"library becomes available.\n"
"\n"
"If you find areas housing fluctuating between two levels regularly, it "
"may be because your population is growing to a level that your "
"libraries can't cover well, which causes land value for some houses to "
"be limited.  When these houses shrink, it lowers the population, which "
"improves the coverage, which causes the houses to grow, which lowers "
"the coverage... and so on.  When this happens, build another library. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#580Help# / #582History#"
msgstr ""
"Votre ville ne peut pas améliorer son habitat tant que vous n'y "
"construisez pas de bibliothèque. Assurez-vous de développer assez votre "
"population pour une bibliothèque.\n"
"\n"
"Si vous constatez que l'habitat fluctue régulièrement entre deux états, "
"c'est que votre population augmente à une vitesse que vos bibliothèques "
"ne supportent pas, ce qui implique la limitation du coût foncier de "
"quelques maisons. Lorsque des maisons disparaissent, la population "
"diminue, ce qui améliore la couverture, les maisons se développent... "
"et ainsi de suite. Lorsque cela se produit, construisez une nouvelle "
"bibliothèque. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#580Aide# / #582Histoire#"

msgctxt "help/582/0"
msgid "Education in Rome"
msgstr "L'éducation"

msgctxt "help/582/1"
msgid ""
"Romans as a whole were a well-educated people, although their area of "
"knowledge was narrow and tightly constrained by what was considered "
"\"proper\" for a Roman citizen to study. Writing, debate, and philosophy "
"qualified; the more technical fields of knowledge such as science, "
"medicine, and the higher maths did not.\n"
"\n"
"Literacy was more common in ancient Rome than at any other time before "
"the nineteenth century.  All of the patrician class and many of the "
"upper middle-class plebeians had their children schooled.  Children "
"started their studies at age seven, usually being tutored by a teacher "
"or an educated Greek slave \n"
"#583Next Page#\n"
"#580Help#/#581Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Les romains, dans l'ensemble, étaient des gens instruits, bien que "
"leurs domaines de connaissances fussent étroits et limités par ce qui "
"était considéré comme assez correct pour être étudié par un citoyen "
"romain. L'écriture, le débat et la philosophie étaient acceptés, des "
"domaines plus techniques tels que la science, la médecine ou les "
"mathématiques ne l'étaient pas.\n"
"La littérature fut plus commune dans la Rome Antique qu'elle le fut "
"jamais avant le dix-neuvième siècle. Les enfants de tous les Patriciens "
"et de la plupart des classes moyennes étaient scolarisés. Les études "
"commençaient à l'âge de sept ans, les cours étant généralement "
"dispensés par un professeur ou un esclave Grec éduqué\n"
"#583Page Suivante#\n"
"#580Aide#/#581Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/583/0"
msgid "Education in Rome (cont.)"
msgstr "L'éducation (suite)"

msgctxt "help/583/1"
msgid ""
"in someone's home or in a public location, which might even be "
"outdoors. Boys and girls were both taught, although in separate "
"classrooms. Early studies primarily covered reading and writing in both "
"Latin and Greek. Discipline was harsh, and beatings for misbehavior "
"were common.  \n"
"\n"
"At age twelve or thirteen, boys went to the Grammaticus, a school where "
"literature, history, elocution and some elementary mathematics "
"(arithmetic and geometry) were taught. Girls might also go the "
"Grammaticus, though less frequently than their brothers, to be taught "
"poetry and music.  Few if any girls advanced to the next level of "
"school, the Rhetor, where students practiced the skills they would need "
"for life as an upper-class Roman: public speaking, debate, and law.  "
"After completing studies \n"
"#582Previous Page#/#584Next Page#\n"
"#580Help#/#581Tips#"
msgstr ""
"dans une maison particulière ou un lieu public, parfois même dehors. "
"Les garçons et les filles étaient également concernés, mais leurs "
"classes étaient séparées. Les études primaires enseignaient la lecture, "
"l'écriture, le Latin et le Grec. A l'âge de douze ou treize ans, les "
"garçons allaient à l'école de Grammaire, une école où l'on apprenait la "
"littérature, l'histoire, l'élocution et quelques principes "
"mathématiques de base. Les filles pouvaient également rejoindre ce type "
"d'école bien qu'elles les fréquentaient moins que leurs frères; on leur "
"apprenait la poésie et la musique. Peu, voire aucune fille, "
"n'atteignant le niveau suivant, l'école de rhétorique, où les étudiants "
"devaient acquérir les talents qui leurs serviraient dans leur vie de "
"Romain appartenant à l'Elite: parler en public, débattre et les lois. A "
"la fin de leurs études,\n"
"#582Page Précédente#/#584Page Suivante#\n"
"#580Aide#/#581Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/584/0"
msgid "Education in Rome (cont.)"
msgstr "L'éducation (suite)"

msgctxt "help/584/1"
msgid ""
"at the Rhetor, a particularly bright or rich student might travel to "
"Athens to study with the famous Greek teachers there. The first Roman "
"\"university\", known as the Athenaeum, was built by the Emperor Hadrian "
"in the second century AD.\n"
"\n"
" Rome also had many libraries, public and private, where scrolls, "
"parchment, and papyrus were stored in wall niches.  By the end of the "
"Empire, there were around forty public libraries and hundreds more "
"private ones in the city. They were built according to a standard floor "
"plan, featuring two rectangular rooms, one for Latin works and one for "
"Greek ones. These libraries functioned much as modern ones do, even "
"extending borrowing privileges to members.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#583Previous Page#\n"
"#580Help#/#581Tips#"
msgstr ""
"à l'école de rhétorique, un étudiant particulièrement brillant ou riche "
"pouvait partir pour Athènes pour y étudier avec les meilleurs "
"professeurs grecs. La première \"université\" romaine, connue sous le nom "
"d'Athenaéum, fut construite par l'empereur Hadrien au second siècle "
"après JC.\n"
"Rome possédait également de nombreuses bibliothèques où des parchemins "
"et des papyrus étaient stockés dans des niches pratiquées dans les "
"murs. A la fin de l'Empire, on dénombrait quarante bibliothèques "
"publiques et une centaine à vocation privée. Elles étaient construites "
"selon un plan standard, composées de deux salles rectangulaires, une "
"pour les ouvrages en latin, l'autre pour ceux en grec. Ces "
"bibliothèques fonctionnaient selon le mode moderne, accordant le "
"privilège des prêts aux membres.\n"
"#583Page Précédente#\n"
"#580Aide#/#581Trucs#"

