msgctxt "help/640/0"
msgid "Hospitals"
msgstr "Les Hôpitaux"

msgctxt "help/640/1"
msgid ""
"Hospitals serve the entire city, and not just the areas near them. Your "
"city's Hospital Coverage is expressed as a percentage, and is displayed "
"in each building's Query panel. If your Hospital Cover dips below 100 "
"percent, housing throughout your city may become diseased. In this "
"case, you should build another hospital.\n"
"\n"
"Hospitals need road access. They also must be within areas that have "
"tax coverage by a Forum, so that Forum officials may administer the "
"hospital.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#641Tips# / #642History#"
msgstr ""
"Les hôpitaux desservent toute la ville au lieu d'une zone voisine. La "
"couverture de l'hôpital est exprimée sous forme de pourcentage. Si le "
"taux de couverture est inférieur à 100%, les habitants sont frappés par "
"la maladie, il est donc temps d'en construire un nouveau. \n"
"\n"
"L'hôpital nécessite un accès routier et doit être dans la zone de "
"recouvrement des taxes d'un Forum, pour que les officiels du Forum "
"puissent l'administrer.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#641Trucs# / #642Histoire#"

msgctxt "help/641/0"
msgid "Tips for Hospitals"
msgstr "Trucs: les Hôpitaux"

msgctxt "help/641/1"
msgid ""
"If you find areas of housing fluctuating between two levels regularly, "
"it may be because your population is growing to a level that your "
"hospitals can't cover well, which causes land value for some houses to "
"be limited.  When these houses shrink, it lowers the population, which "
"improves the coverage, which causes the houses to grow, which lowers "
"the coverage... and so on.  When this happens, build another hospital. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#640Help# / #642History#"
msgstr ""
"Si vous constatez que l'habitat fluctue régulièrement entre deux états, "
"c'est que votre population augmente à une vitesse que vos hôpitaux ne "
"supportent pas, ce qui implique la limitation du coût foncier de "
"quelques maisons. Lorsque des maisons disparaissent, la population "
"diminue, ce qui améliore la couverture, les maisons se développent... "
"et ainsi de suite. Lorsque cela se produit, construisez un nouvel "
"hôpital. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#640Aide# / #642Histoire#"

msgctxt "help/642/0"
msgid "Roman Health"
msgstr "La santé Romaine"

msgctxt "help/642/1"
msgid ""
"Given the technological restrictions of the ancient world, the city of "
"Rome was a remarkably clean and healthful place to live.  The Romans "
"highly valued cleanliness, both personal and civic.  City cleanliness "
"was accomplished through Roman civil engineering, while personal "
"cleanliness was provided for by means of public bath houses.  \n"
"\n"
" Rome's site on the river Tiber is not a particularly good location in "
"which to build a city.  The hills are steep, and the valleys between "
"them are marshy and have a tendency to flood when the river is high.  "
"The marshes, with their bountiful malarial mosquito population, "
"presented the greatest threat to Roman \n"
"#643Next Page#\n"
"#640Help#/#641Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Malgré les restrictions technologiques de l'Antiquité, la Cité de Rome "
"était un endroit de vie propre et sain. Les romains plaçaient très haut "
"la propreté, à la fois individuelle et civique. La propreté de la Cité "
"était entretenue par le génie civil Romain , la propreté individuelle "
"étant l'affaire des Thermes.\n"
"\n"
"L'emplacement de Rome sur le Tibre n'est pas le meilleur endroit pour "
"bâtir une ville. Les collines sont escarpées, les vallées marécageuses "
"et ont tendance à l'inondation en cas de crue du fleuve. Les marécages, "
"avec leurs colonies de moustiques porteurs de malaria, étaient le plus "
"grand risque pour la santé des Romains.\n"
"#643Page Suivante#\n"
"#640Aide#/#641Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/643/0"
msgid "Roman Health (cont.)"
msgstr "La santé Romaine (suite)"

msgctxt "help/643/1"
msgid ""
"health. The Romans, of course, didn't understand where malaria came "
"from, but they could see that swamps weren't healthy places to live.  "
"Accordingly, they built huge drains to channel water away from the land "
"(the Cloaca Maxima being the oldest example still in existence), and "
"created the Pool of the Tiber to control river flooding.   They also "
"improved their chances for health by importing fresh spring water via "
"aqueducts, rather than drinking from the polluted river.  In addition, "
"most of Rome had a well-developed sewer system,  (sometimes "
"underground, sometimes just as street channels to take away refuse) "
"except for the poorer quarters, where people simply threw refuse into "
"the streets.  There were also more than 150 well-drained public "
"lavatories. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#642Previous Page#/#644Next Page#\n"
"#640Help#/#641Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Bien entendu, les Romains ne savaient pas d'où provenait la malaria, "
"mais ils pouvaient comprendre que les marais n'étaient pas les endroits "
"les plus sains qu'il soit. Ils construisirent alors d'immenses égouts "
"pour évacuer l'eau (le Cloaca Maxima en est l'exemple le plus ancien), "
"ils créèrent la bassin du Tibre pour maîtriser les crues. Ils "
"minimisèrent les risques pour leur santé en apportant l'eau fraîche des "
"sources par les aqueducs, au lieu de boire l'eau polluée du fleuve. De "
"plus la majeure partie de Rome avait un système d'égouts très "
"développé,(parfois souterrain, parfois sous forme de caniveaux dans les "
"rues qui entraînaient les détritus), sauf dans les quartiers pauvres où "
"les gens jetaient simplement leurs détritus dans les rues. On trouvait "
"également plus de 150 toilettes publiques bien canalisées.\n"
"\n"
"#642Page Précédente#/#644Page Suivante#\n"
"#640Aide#/#641Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/644/0"
msgid "Roman Health (cont.)"
msgstr "La santé Romaine (suite)"

msgctxt "help/644/1"
msgid ""
"Rome was a much healthier place to live under the Imperium than at any "
"time during the Middle Ages or Renaissance, and many of the other "
"cities in Italy and elsewhere in the Imperium also benefited from Roman "
"knowledge of sanitation.  Given the basic lack of knowledge about "
"disease causes, however, the threat of plague could never be eliminated "
"entirely, particularly in the crowded conditions of Rome, and the "
"mobility provided by the Roman road system made it easy for foreigners "
"to bring new diseases to Italy from far-off lands. Disease struck the "
"city several times in its history with the usual severity of ancient "
"plagues; in one outbreak during the reign of Marcus Aurelius, it was "
"estimated that 2,000 people died every day. \n"
"\n"
" Public hospitals were provided, but medical science, \n"
"#643Previous Page#/#645Next Page#\n"
"#640Help#/#641Tips#"
msgstr ""
"Il fut plus sain de vivre à Rome sous l'Empire qu'à toute autre époque "
"durant le Moyen-Age ou la Renaissance et bien d'autres villes, en "
"Italie ou dans le reste de l'Empire, bénéficièrent du savoir-faire "
"Romain sur le plan sanitaire. Compte tenu du manque de connaissances de "
"base sur l'origine des maladies, la menace de la peste ne fut jamais "
"totalement éliminée, particulièrement dans la Rome surpeuplée, et la "
"mobilité due au système de routes Romain provoqua l'arrivée en Italie "
"de nouvelles maladies venues de terres lointaines. Les maladies "
"frappèrent la ville à plusieurs reprises au cours de son histoire, avec "
"la cruauté habituelle des pestes antiques, lorsqu'elle se déclara sous "
"le règne de Marc Aurèle, on estima le nombre de morts à 2 000 par jour.\n"
"Les hôpitaux publics se développaient mais la médecine,\n"
"#643Page Précédente#/#645Page Suivante#\n"
"#640Aide#/#641Trucs#"

msgctxt "help/645/0"
msgid "Roman Health (cont.)"
msgstr "La santé Romaine (suite)"

msgctxt "help/645/1"
msgid ""
"like all other sciences under the Romans, except civil engineering and "
"animal husbandry, stagnated -- the health care available at the end of "
"the Imperium was not much better than that found at the start of the "
"Republic.  Proper Romans also did not consider a physician's work to be "
"dignified enough for a true Roman to undertake; ergo, nearly all the "
"doctors in the city were Greek, some of them slaves. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#644Previous Page#\n"
"#640Help#/#641Tips#"
msgstr ""
"comme toutes les autres sciences chez les Romains, excepté la Génie "
"Civil et l'élevage, stagnait -- la prévention médicale à la fin de "
"L'Empire n'était pas meilleure qu'elle ne l'était au début de la "
"République. Les Romains comme il faut considéraient que le travail de "
"physicien n'était pas digne d'être entrepris par un vrai Romain, donc, "
"presque tous les docteurs de la Cité étaient des Grecs, certains étant "
"esclaves.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#644Page Précédente#\n"
"#640Aide#/#641Trucs#"

