msgctxt "help/1264/0"
msgid "The Second Triumvirate"
msgstr "Le Second Triumvirat"

msgctxt "help/1264/1"
msgid ""
"Octavian's first action was to attempt to claim Caesar's estate from "
"Marc Antony, but Antony, who had already spent much of the money, "
"ignored him. Undaunted, Octavius sold some of his own property to raise "
"money, most of which he spent ingratiating himself with Caesar's "
"troops. In this he had an edge over his rival, for the personal loyalty "
"of Caesar's legions to their fallen commander required that they honor "
"Caesar's wishes as expressed in his will. Since Caesar had chosen "
"Octavius, not Marc Antony, Caesar's soldiers were inclined to prefer "
"the teenager as well, despite the fact that he had not yet proven "
"himself in combat. Octavian also found himself unexpectedly favored by "
"the Senate, who considered him less of a threat than the experienced "
"Antony. This esteemed body even went so far as to name Octavian a "
"senator and give him a vacant consulship, \n"
"#1263Previous Page# / #1265 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"La première action d'Octave fut de tenter de réclamer les propriétés "
"foncières de Marc Antoine, mais Antoine qui avait déjà dépensé beaucoup "
"d'argent, l'ignorait. Intrépide, Octave vendit quelques-unes de ses "
"propriétés pour disposer des liquidités, qu'il dépensa en intégrant les "
"troupes de César. Il y détenait alors une position avantageuse par "
"rapport à son rival; par loyauté envers leurs ancien chef, les légions "
"de César voulaient respecter les dernières volontés de celui-ci. "
"Puisque César avait chois Octavius et non Marc Antoine, les soldats de "
"César préféraient le jeune homme malgré son manque d'expérience dans le "
"combat. Octave était également de façon surprenante, soutenu par le "
"Sénat, qui le considérait moins menaçant que l'expérimenté Antoine. "
"L'estimable corps allait même jusqu'à le nommer sénateur et à lui "
"donner un consulat vacant,\n"
"#1263Page Précédente# / #1265 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1265/0"
msgid "The Second Triumvirate (Cont.)"
msgstr "Le Second Triumvirat (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1265/1"
msgid ""
"though he was far too young to legally hold either position. The Senate "
"no doubt planned to make a puppet ruler out of Octavian to appease the "
"masses. Octavian had other plans.\n"
"\n"
"Aware that he had no way of asserting his dominance over Marc Antony "
"just yet, Octavian proposed a novel solution probably inspired by "
"Caesar's alliance with Crassus and Pompey. He, Marc Antony, and Lepidus "
"(another Caesar supporter and chief priest of Rome) would rule the "
"Roman Empire jointly as the Second Triumvirate, starting in 43 BC. "
"Antony, who had experience and money but did not have Octavian's "
"legitimacy, agreed, and Lepidus, who otherwise would have had nothing, "
"was quite happy to join. Lepidus's participation in the Triumvirate was "
"no more than ceremonial; the real power rested with \n"
"#1264Previous Page# / #1266 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"bien qu'il ait été trop jeune pour occuper ce poste. Le Sénat prévoyait "
"sans nul doute de le manipuler comme une marionnette pour apaiser les "
"masses. Mais Octave leur réservait d'autres plans.\n"
"\n"
"Conscient de n'avoir pas réussi à s'imposer face à Marc Antoine, Octave "
"proposa une solution nouvelle probablement inspirée par l'alliance de "
"César avec Crassus et Pompée. Lui, Marc Antoine, et Lepidus (un autre "
"supporter de César, chef des prêtres à Rome), régneraient ensemble sur "
"l'Empire romain comme le Second Triumvirat, qui débuta en 43 av. J.-C. "
"Antoine, qui avait possédait expérience et fortune, mais n'avait pas la "
"légitimité d'Octave, accepta et Lepidus qui n'avait rien, était heureux "
"de s'y joindre. La participation de Lepidus dans le Triumvirat était "
"purement cérémonial ; le vrai pouvoir demeurait dans les mains\n"
"#1264Page Précédente# / #1266 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1266/0"
msgid "The Second Triumvirate (Cont.)"
msgstr "Le Second Triumvirat (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1266/1"
msgid ""
"Antony and Octavian. The Senate, with little else in the way of useful "
"options, agreed to give the Triumvirate legal status for a period of "
"five years, \"for the reconstitution of the state.\" The first act of the "
"Triumvirs was to execute 300 senators and 2,000 citizens of lower rank. "
"No doubt both Antony and Octavian were eager to avoid Caesar's mistake "
"of leaving his enemies alive.\n"
"\n"
"In 42 the Senate voted to award Julius Caesar the status of a god of "
"the state, and gave the deified dictator the title \"Julius Divus.\" Why "
"exactly they did this is unclear. Perhaps they hoped to win the favor "
"of the Triumvirs and prevent further Senatorial executions. They also "
"may have anticipated how this action would add to Octavian's prestige "
"(at Antony's expense) by giving him the status of the son of a god. \n"
"#1265Previous Page# / #1267 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"d'Antoine et d'Octave. Le Sénat, qui n'avait que peu de marge de "
"manoeuvre, accepta de donner au Triumvirat un statut légal pour une "
"période de cinq ans, \"pour la reconstitution de l'état\". La première "
"action des Triumvirs fut d'exécuter 300 sénateurs et 2.000 citoyens de "
"rang inférieur. Aucun doute que Antoine et Octave voulaient éviter les "
"erreurs de César en gardant ses ennemis en vie.\n"
"\n"
"En 42 le Sénat vota l'attribution du statut de dieu de l'état à Julius "
"Caesar, et donna au dictateur déifié le titre de \"Jumlius Divus\". Leur "
"intention en faisant cela reste encore ambigu. Peut-être espéraient-ils "
"gagner la faveur du Triumvirs et éviter d'éventuelles exécutions. Ils "
"ont pu également pensé que cette action augmenterait le prestige "
"d'Octave  (au dépend de celui d'Antoine) en lui octroyant le statut de "
"fils d'un dieu. \n"
"#1265Page Précédente# / #1267 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1267/0"
msgid "The Second Triumvirate (Cont.)"
msgstr "Le Second Triumvirat (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1267/1"
msgid ""
"Octavian promptly had a fine temple built and dedicated to his deified "
"adoptive father.\n"
"\n"
"After dealing with their enemies in Rome, Octavian and Antony sailed "
"east with a fleet of warships to deal with Caesar's assassins. They "
"engaged the forces of Cassius and Brutus twice at Philippi, winning "
"each time; after the second loss, the two leading conspirators took "
"their own lives. That vital business dealt with, Octavian and Antony "
"returned to Rome and spent three years running the Empire and "
"attempting to strengthen their political positions. Octavian went off "
"to fight several small wars in order to gain experience in combat and "
"the prestige necessary to improve his status with the Legions.\n"
"\n"
"In 38 BC the Triumvirs gathered at Brundisium to \n"
"#1266Previous Page# / #1268 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Octave bâtit rapidement un splendide temple et le dédia à son père "
"adoptif.\n"
"\n"
"Après s'être occupés de leurs ennemis à Rome, Octave et Antoine "
"embarquèrent vers l'est avec une flotte de vaisseaux de guerre pour "
"poursuivre les assassins de César. Ils attaquèrent les forces Cassius "
"et Brutus deux fois à Philippi, et remportèrent à deux fois le combat ; "
"après leur seconde défaite, les deux conspirateurs sauvèrent leur "
"propre vie. Cette tâche importante réglée, Octave et Antoine "
"retournèrent à Rome et passèrent trois ans à diriger l'Empire et à "
"s'efforcer de renforcer leur positions politiques.\n"
"\n"
"En 38 av. J.-C., le Triumvirat se rassembla à Brundisium pour\n"
"#1266Page Précédente# / #1268 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1268/0"
msgid "The Second Triumvirate (Cont.)"
msgstr "Le Second Triumvirat (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1268/1"
msgid ""
"renew their alliance. At this time, they divided the Roman lands among "
"themselves so that each could have a sphere of absolute authority. "
"Octavian took the West, Antony got the East, and Lepidus got a small "
"part of Africa to keep him happy. Italy was supposed to remain neutral, "
"but with Antony off enjoying the riches of Egypt and Lepidus "
"politically ineffectual, the heartland of the Empire was effectively in "
"Octavian's hands.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1267Previous Page# / #1269 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"renouer leur alliance. Cette fois, ils se partagèrent les terres "
"romaines afin d'arranger des sphères d'autorité indépendantes. Octave "
"prit l'Ouest, Antoine eut l'Est et Lepidus eut une petite partie de "
"l'Afrique. L'Italie était censée rester neutre, mais comme Antoine "
"voulait profiter les richesses d'Egypte et Lepidus politiquement "
"inapte, le coeur de l'Empire était effectivement dans les mains "
"d'Octave.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1267Page Précédente# / #1269 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

