msgctxt "help/1291/0"
msgid "The Julio-Claudians (Cont.)"
msgstr "Les Julio-Claudiens (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1291/1"
msgid ""
"The long reign of Augustus was responsible for allowing his reforms to "
"take firm root throughout the Empire, but it presented him with a "
"problem of a different sort: he outlived all of his first choices for a "
"successor. His only surviving child was a daughter, Julia. Augustus "
"married her off to his intended heir, his nephew Marcellus. Marcellus "
"died unexpectedly, however, before fathering any children, so Julia was "
"married off again, this time to Marcus Agrippa. This marriage was more "
"successful, producing five children, including two promising boys named "
"Gaius and Lucius. But both boys died before Augustus did, Lucius of an "
"illness and Gaius a few years later in battle.\n"
"\n"
"Left without a better choice, Augustus settled on his stepson Tiberius, "
"child of his second wife Livia by her \n"
"#1290Previous Page# / #1292 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Le long règne d'Auguste a permis à ses réformes de prendre racine à "
"travers l'Empire, mais il lui présentait un problème d'un autre genre : "
"il a survécu à tous ses héritiers. Son seul enfant encore en vie était "
"sa fille, Julia. Auguste la maria à son héritier prévu, son neveu "
"Marcellus. Marcellus mourut de façon inattendue, avant même de pouvoir "
"être père, et Julia se maria de nouveau, cette fois, à Marcus Agrippa. "
"Ce mariage fut plus heureux, et donna naissance à cinq enfants, dont "
"deux garçons à l'avenir prometteur, Gaius et Lucius. Mais les deux "
"garçons moururent avant Auguste, Lucius de maladie et Gaius quelques "
"années plus tard lors d'une bataille.\n"
"\n"
"N'ayant pas de meilleur choix, Auguste présenta son beau-fils Tibère, "
"le fils que sa seconde femme Lyvia\n"
"#1290Page Précédente# / #1292 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1292/0"
msgid "The Julio-Claudians (Cont.)"
msgstr "Les Julio-Claudiens (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1292/1"
msgid ""
"first husband. There were worse choices, to be sure, but Tiberius's "
"reign as Emperor would not be a happy one. He was undoubtedly the best "
"of a bad lot. Unfortunately, that is about the most one can say of him.\n"
"\n"
"A HARD ACT TO FOLLOW\n"
"\n"
"Tiberius was an intelligent man and an excellent soldier, but he was "
"temperamentally unsuited for the role Augustus forced on him. In order "
"to make him a potential heir, Augustus ordered him to divorce his first "
"wife (whom he genuinely loved) and enter into a disastrous union with "
"Augustus's twice-widowed daughter Julia. Julia, also unhappy about the "
"marriage, expressed that feeling with a long series of affairs that she "
"didn't bother to hide from her new \n"
"#1291Previous Page# / #1293 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"avait eu avec un premier mari. Il aurait pu tomber sur pire certes, "
"mais le règne de Tibère en tant qu'Empereur ne fut pas très réussi. "
"C'était indubitablement le meilleur parmi des mauvais. Malheureusement, "
"c'était son seul mérite.\n"
"\n"
"UN ACTE DIFFICILE A SUIVRE\n"
"\n"
"Tibère était un homme intelligent et un excellent soldat, mais il ne "
"convenait pas du tout au rôle que lui avait assigné Auguste. Afin d'en "
"faire un hériter potentiel, Auguste lui ordonna de divorcer de sa "
"première femme, qu'il aimait tendrement, pour contracter une union "
"désastreuse avec la fille d'Auguste, Julia. Julia, malheureuse de ce "
"mariage, exprima son sentiment par une longue série d'aventures qu'elle "
"ne prenait même pas la peine de cacher.\n"
"#1291Page Précédente# / #1293 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1293/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr "Un acte difficile à suivre (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1293/1"
msgid ""
"husband. Under Roman law Tiberius would have been perfectly justified "
"in divorcing her, but Augustus was blind to his daughter's behavior, "
"and to denounce her to the Emperor would be a dangerous move. Tiberius "
"dealt with the situation by going into self-imposed exile on Rhodes, "
"leaving Julia behind in Rome to indulge herself.\n"
"\n"
"On Rhodes, the general who had shown remarkable care and concern for "
"his soldiers in the field changed into an angry, bitter, and withdrawn "
"man who seemed to care for no one and nothing beyond himself and his "
"own pleasures. Although he had gone into exile voluntarily, he could "
"not return to Rome until Augustus granted him permission, and Augustus "
"seemed disinclined to do so. Then a series of personal disasters befell "
"Augustus. First, his wife Livia secured \n"
"#1292Previous Page# / #1294 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Selon la loi romaine, Tibère aurait pu obtenir légitimement le divorce, "
"mais Augustus restait aveugle devant les agissements de sa fille, et la "
"dénoncer à l'Empereur eut été une tentative dangereuse. Tibère régla la "
"situation en s'exilant volontairement sur Rhodes, laissant Julia à "
"elle-même à Rome.\n"
"\n"
"A Rhodes, le général qui avait montré une attention remarquable à "
"l'égard de ses soldats, devint en un homme en colère, aigri et replié "
"sur soi-même qui ne se préoccupait plus de personne ni de rien à part "
"sa propre personne et ses plaisirs. Bien qu'il s'était exiler "
"volontairement, il ne pouvait pas retourner à Rome tant qu'Auguste ne "
"lui en avait pas donné la permission, et Auguste semblait peu enclin à "
"le faire. Une série de désastres personnels frappa ensuite Auguste.\n"
"#1292Page Précédente# / #1294 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1294/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr "Un acte difficile à suivre (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1294/1"
msgid ""
"proof of Julia's adultery and presented it to the Emperor; under Roman "
"law, Julia should have been executed, but instead Augustus banished her "
"forever to a small Mediterranean island. Then Julia's son Lucius died, "
"leaving only Gaius as a possible successor to the Imperial seat. "
"Augustus, seeing his list of heirs shortened, recalled Tiberius from "
"Rhodes.\n"
"\n"
"When Gaius died in combat, Tiberius was the closest thing to legitimate "
"successor left to Augustus. By then old and ill, Augustus named "
"Tiberius his adopted son and heir. He never liked Tiberius, however, "
"and Tiberius hated him. When Augustus finally passed on in 14 AD, "
"Tiberius tried to coerce the Senate into denying the deceased Emperor "
"the status of godhood. He failed, however, and Augustus ascended to sit \n"
"\n"
"#1293Previous Page# / #1295 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"D'abord, sa femme Lyvia qui détenait des preuves concernant les "
"affaires adultères de Julia, les présenta à l'Empereur; selon la loi "
"romaine, Julia aurait dû être exécutée, mais au lieu de cela, Auguste "
"l'exila à vie sur une petite île méditerranéenne. Le fils de Julia, "
"Lucius mourut ensuite, laissant Gaius comme seul successeur possible au "
"trône impérial. Auguste, voyant que le nombre de ses héritiers "
"diminuait, rappela Tibère de Rhodes.\n"
"\n"
"Quand Gaius mourut au combat, Tibère devenait le successeur le plus "
"légitime d'Auguste. Agé et malade, Auguste adopta Tibère et le désigna "
"comme héritier. Mais il n'avait jamais aimé Tibère et celui-ci le "
"haïssait. Quand Auguste rendit enfin l'âme en 14 ap. J.-C., Tibère "
"tenta de convaincre le Sénat de ne pas donner le statut de dieu au "
"défunt Empereur. Il échoua et Auguste eut sa place\n"
"#1293Page Précédente# / #1295 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1295/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr "Un acte difficile à suivre (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1295/1"
msgid ""
"beside Julius Caesar in the pantheon of the state gods of Rome.\n"
"\n"
"No doubt Tiberius would have been much happier as a simple Roman "
"general, living with his beloved first wife, instead of being dragged "
"unwillingly to the Imperial seat. At first, however, he ruled well "
"enough; his new laws were sensible, fair, and forward-looking, and "
"throughout his reign he was careful about the Empire's economic "
"security. From the beginning, though, the number of treason "
"accusations, trials, and confiscations of property showed an ominous "
"increase that did not bode well for the future. Without any legal "
"protection against the whims of the Emperor, the notables of Rome could "
"only keep their heads low, cross their fingers, and hope to escape "
"notice.\n"
"\n"
"#1294Previous Page# / #1296 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"aux côtés de Julius Caesar dans le panthéon des dieux de l'état de Rome.\n"
"\n"
"Il ne faisait aucun doute que Tibère aurait été bien plus heureux en "
"tant que simple général romain, restant avec sa première femme adorée, "
"au lieu d'être poussé sur le siège impérial. A première vue, il "
"gouverna avec succès; ses nouvelles lois étaient morales, justes et "
"clairvoyantes, et pendant tout son règne, il se préoccupa beaucoup de "
"la sécurité économique de l'Empire. Dès le début cependant, un certain "
"nombre d'accusations de trahisons, de procès, et de confiscations de "
"propriétés tendaient vers une escalade qui ne présageaient rien de bon "
"pour l'avenir. Sans protection légale contre les caprices de "
"l'Empereur, les notables de Rome pouvaient seulement baisser la tête, "
"croiser leurs doigts et espérer passer au travers des mailles du filet.\n"
"#1294Page Précédente# / #1296 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1296/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr "Un acte difficile à suivre (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1296/1"
msgid ""
"It is unclear whether or not Tiberius was paranoid, or whether people "
"really were out to get him. By the time he succeeded Augustus, he had "
"become a sullen, bitter, and self-absorbed man, with little ability to "
"make himself liked, and less interest in doing so. Augustus had "
"appeased the Senate by asking their advice and treating them with "
"respect even as he took away the real power that had been theirs, but "
"Tiberius disdained the Senate and did not try to conceal the fact. The "
"feeling was mutual. Under such circumstances, paranoia on Tiberius's "
"part was understandable. It was also understandable, however, that a "
"Senate threatened with treason charges on little to no evidence might "
"begin to plot in earnest.\n"
"\n"
"In 23 AD Tiberius's only son and intended successor Drusus died under "
"suspicious circumstances, and \n"
"#1295Previous Page# / #1297 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"On ne sait pas si Tibère était paranoïaque ou non, ou si les gens ne "
"l'aimaient vraiment pas. Au moment où il succéda à Auguste, il devint "
"un homme renfrogné, aigri et égoïste, incapable du moindre charisme. "
"Auguste avait apaisé le Sénat en lui demandant conseil et en les "
"traitant avec respect même quand il disposait de plus de pouvoirs que "
"lui, mais Tibère dédaignait le Sénat et ne s'en cacha même pas. Le "
"sentiment était partagé. Dans de telles circonstances, la paranoïa de "
"Tibère était partiellement compréhensible. Ce qui l'était également, "
"c'est qu'un sénateur accusé de trahison avait besoin d'un complot "
"sérieux.\n"
"\n"
"En 23 ap. J.-C., l'unique fils et héritier de Tibère, Drusus, mourut "
"dans de mystérieuses circonstances, et\n"
"#1295Page Précédente# / #1297 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1297/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr "Un acte difficile à suivre (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1297/1"
msgid ""
"Tiberius became even more embittered. In 26 AD he took up residence on "
"the island of Capri, which he made into his personal retreat. He left "
"the job of running the Empire to his trusted Praetorian Guard captain, "
"Sejanus. Tiberius's abandonment of Rome offended the Senate, and "
"relations between the Senate and the Emperor deteriorated even further. "
"On Capri, Tiberius's anger and paranoia appear to have developed into "
"full-blown madness; his private residence became the site of bizarre "
"and perverse acts of torture and depravity.\n"
"\n"
"Sejanus apparently held aspirations to take the Imperial seat for "
"himself. In 31 AD Tiberius received information showing that Sejanus "
"had had an affair with Drusus's wife, and the two of them had conspired "
"to poison Drusus. Tiberius sent a letter to \n"
"#1296Previous Page# / #1298 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Tibère devint encore plus aigri. En 26 ap. J.-C., il s'installa sur "
"l'île de Capri, dont il fit sa retraite personnelle. Il laissa ses "
"fonctions de gouvernement à un capitaine de la Garde Prétorienne en qui "
"il avait confiance, Sejanus. L'abandon de Tibère offensa le Sénat, et "
"les relations entre le Sénat et l'Empereur se détériorèrent. A Capri, "
"la colère et la paranoïa de Tibère semblent avoir gagné en intensité; "
"sa résidence privée devenait le site d'actes bizarres et pervers de "
"tortures et de dépravation.\n"
"\n"
"Sejanus ambitionnait apparemment d'accéder au trône impérial. En 31 ap. "
"J.-C., Tibère apprit que Séjanus avait une liaison avec la femme de "
"Drusus, et que les deux avait conspiré pour empoisonner Drusus. Tibère "
"envoya une lettre\n"
"#1296Page Précédente# / #1298 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1298/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr "Un acte difficile à suivre (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1298/1"
msgid ""
"the Senate that began by praising Sejanus, and ended by calling for his "
"immediate execution. The Senate complied. But this act of treachery "
"soured Tiberius even further on Rome. Without a son for an heir, "
"Tiberius decided to get his revenge on Rome through his successor.\n"
"\n"
"Tiberius's choice was his young grand-nephew, Gaius Germanicus. "
"Germanicus's father had been a famous general, and the boy himself had "
"grown up among the soldiers and was popular with them. His personality "
"flaws were significant, however, a fact which Tiberius seems to have "
"noted with glee: \"I am nursing a viper in Rome's bosom,\" he is reported "
"to have said. Time would prove him right. Gaius Germanicus became "
"better known by the nickname \n"
"\n"
"#1297Previous Page# / #1299 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"au Sénat, qui commençait par faire l'éloge de Séjanus, et qui finissait "
"par la demande de son exécution immédiate. Le Sénat s'y conforma. Mais "
"cet acte de trahison aigrissait encore plus Tibère contre Rome. Sans "
"héritier, Tibère décida de se venger de Rome grâce à son successeur.\n"
"\n"
"Le choix de Tibère se porta sur son petit neveu, le jeune Gaius "
"Germanicus. Le père de Germanicus avait été un célèbre général, et le "
"garçon lui-même avait grandi parmi les soldats et était populaire parmi "
"ceux-ci. Ses imperfections étaient cependant évidentes et Tibère "
"lui-même les avait remarquées :\"j'ai élevé une vipère dans le sein de "
"Rome \"avait-il l'habitude de dire. Le temps lui donnerait raison. Gaius "
"Germanicus est tristement célèbre par un surnom\n"
"\n"
"#1297Page Précédente# / #1299 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1299/0"
msgid "A Hard Act to Follow (Cont.)"
msgstr "Un acte difficile à suivre (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1299/1"
msgid ""
"given to him by the soldiers as a child: Caligula, meaning \"Little "
"Boot,\" after the tiny army boots he wore.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1298Previous Page# / #1300 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"que lui avaient donné les soldat quand il était enfant : Caligula, ce "
"qui signifie \"Petite Botte\", à cause des bottes minuscules qu'il "
"portait.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1298Page Précédente# / #1300 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

