msgctxt "help/1301/0"
msgid "Tiberius's Viper"
msgstr "La vipère de Tibère"

msgctxt "help/1301/1"
msgid ""
"The events of Caligula's succession set the tone for the reign that was "
"to follow. In 37 AD, Tiberius became ill and fell into a coma. "
"Physicians were called to Capri, where they declared that the Emperor "
"would not live out the night. In Rome, Caligula's ascension was "
"announced, the legions hailed him, and the city proclaimed its new "
"leader. Then Tiberius awoke, sat up, and asked for something to eat. "
"Thinking fast, the captain of the Praetorian Guard picked up the "
"bedclothes and smothered Tiberius with them.\n"
"\n"
"Caligula's reign is a matter of some controversy among historians. "
"There's no doubt that it was bad, but exactly how bad it was and why "
"remain in doubt. Historians of ancient Rome had a virulent hatred of "
"Caligula, which he probably earned, but \n"
"#1300Previous Page# / #1302 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Les événements liés à la succession de Caligula donne le ton pour le "
"règne qui suivit. En 37 ap. J.-C., Tibère tomba malade et tomba dans le "
"coma. Les physiciens (médecins) était appelés à Capri, où ils "
"déclarèrent que l'Empereur ne survivrait pas à la nuit. A Rome, "
"l'ascension de Caligula était annoncée, les légions le saluèrent, et la "
"cité le proclama comme son nouveau chef. Mais Tibère se réveilla, "
"s'assit, et réclama quelque chose à manger. Le capitaine de la Garde "
"Prétorienne prit alors les draps et étouffa Tibère avec.\n"
"\n"
"Le règne de Caligula fait partie des plus controversés parmi les "
"historiens. Il ne fait aucun doute que c'était une mauvais règne mais "
"dont l'ampleur et les raisons restent encore mystérieuses. Les "
"historiens de la Rome antique vouaient une haine virulente envers "
"Caligula, ce qu'il a probablement mérité,\n"
"#1300Page Précédente# / #1302 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1302/0"
msgid "Tiberius's Viper (Cont.)"
msgstr "La vipère de Tibère (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1302/1"
msgid ""
"which led them to make up stories about his reign that weren't true, "
"such as one saying that he made his horse consul. They hardly needed to "
"have bothered, though -- Caligula's reign displayed enough insanity "
"without embellishment to convince anyone that the man should never have "
"been made Emperor.\n"
"\n"
"The succession of Caligula was at first the cause of rejoicing in Rome; "
"Tiberius had been disliked, and Caligula was popular -- for the moment. "
"The new Emperor immediately threw open the Imperial treasury that "
"Tiberius had carefully husbanded and indulged in lavish parties, games, "
"and other public entertainments. About seven months into his reign, "
"however, he was struck with a sudden and severe illness that almost "
"killed him. When he recovered, he \n"
"#1301Previous Page# / #1303 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"mais la plupart des histoires qu'on a raconté à propos de son règne "
"étaient fausses, comme celle qui dit qu'il avait fait consul son cheval "
"par exemple. Ils n'avaient pas vraiment besoin de le faire -- le règne "
"de Caligula montre assez d'insanités sans embellissement pour "
"convaincre que l'homme n'aurait jamais du être nommé Empereur.\n"
"\n"
"L'accession de Caligula a d'abord créé l'allégresse à Rome; Tibère "
"n'était pas aimé, et Caligula était populaire -- pour l'instant. Le "
"nouvel Empereur dépensa le trésor impérial que Tibère avait sauvegardé "
"et protégé en prodiguant fêtes, jeux et autres manifestations "
"populaires. Vers le septième mois de son règne néanmoins, il fut frappé "
"d'une soudaine et sévère maladie qui faillit le tuer. Quand il retrouva "
"ses esprits,\n"
"#1301Page Précédente# / #1303 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1303/0"
msgid "Tiberius's Viper (Cont.)"
msgstr "La vipère de Tibère (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1303/1"
msgid ""
"had changed, and for the worse. He may simply have suffered brain "
"damage from his illness and become psychotic. Possibly he became "
"epileptic. Whatever the cause, Tiberius's viper became viciously "
"poisonous.\n"
"\n"
"Treason trials became increasingly frequent and arbitrary. Punishments "
"were unprecedented in their cruelty. Threats of an accusation of "
"treason were used to extort money from wealthy Romans to support "
"Caligula's wasteful spending habits. These became even more pronounced "
"and purposeless; at one point, for example, he decreed that a bridge of "
"boats should be constructed over the bay of Naples. Caligula slept with "
"his sisters and had one of them declared a goddess after her death. He "
"marched his armies on Gaul with little effect, and stirred up trouble \n"
"#1302Previous Page# / #1304 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"il avait changé, et en pire. Il a pu subir des traumatismes au cerveau "
"et devenir psychotique. Il devint probablement épileptique. Quelle "
"qu'en soit la cause, la vipère de Tibère était devenue venimeuse.\n"
"\n"
"Les procès de trahison devinrent fréquents et arbitraires. Les "
"châtiments atteignaient une cruauté sans précédent. Les menaces "
"d'accusation de trahison étaient utilisés pour extorquer de l'argent de "
"la part des Romains riches pour subvenir aux habitudes dépensières de "
"Caligula. Ces caprices devenaient de plus en plus fous et inutiles ; "
"une fois par exemple, il décréta qu'un pont de navires devait être "
"construit au-dessus de la baie de Naples. Caligula coucha avec ses "
"soeurs et proclama l'une d'entre elles déesse après sa mort. Il dirigea "
"vainement son armée sur la Gaule, et créa des troubles \n"
"#1302Page Précédente# / #1304 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1304/0"
msgid "Tiberius's Viper (Cont.)"
msgstr "La vipère de Tibère (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1304/1"
msgid ""
"elsewhere on Rome's borders just for his amusement.\n"
"\n"
"The degree to which Caligula was mad, instead of just vicious, is "
"debatable. As already noted, some of his reported wilder actions were "
"fabricated by his enemies. Other actions may have been intended as "
"\"sympathetic magic.\" For example, he marched his troops to the shore of "
"the English Channel, then ordered them to collect seashells and bring "
"them back to Rome, where he declared that he had conquered the sea. He "
"also had some of his men put on blond wigs and paraded them in Rome, "
"claiming he had conquered Gaul. This may have been insanity, or it may "
"have been intended as a ritual to bring about the conquest of Britain "
"and of Gaul as a concrete reality. The truth is lost in time.\n"
"\n"
"#1303Previous Page# / #1305 Next Page #\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"aux frontières de Rome juste pour s'amuser.\n"
"\n"
"Le niveau de folie et de vice de Caligula est contestable. Comme nous "
"l'avons déjà dit, certaines des actions les plus folles étaient "
"inventées par ses ennemis. D'autres ont pu avoir été effectuées comme "
"des rituels magiques. Par exemple, il dirigea ses troupes sur la shore "
"de la Manche, puis leur ordonna de ramasser des coquillages et de les "
"rapporter à Rome, où il déclara qu'il avait conquis la mer. Il demanda "
"également à ses hommes de mettre des perruques blondes et de parader "
"dans Rome en proclamant qu'il avait conquis la Gaule. Ces actions ont "
"pu être dues à la maladie, ou tout simplement à un respect de rituels "
"censés exaucer les voeux de conquête de la Bretagne et de la Gaule. La "
"vérité est perdue au fil des temps.\n"
"\n"
"#1303Page Précédente# / #1305 Page suivante #\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1305/0"
msgid "Tibererius's Viper (Cont.)"
msgstr "La vipère de Tibère (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1305/1"
msgid ""
"For whatever reasons, Caligula's horrible reign gave Rome a taste of "
"what autocratic rule could be like under a bad ruler. In January of 41, "
"the Romans had had enough; a mob led by the Praetorian Guard and "
"several Senators assassinated Caligula, and then killed his wife and "
"children for good measure. The viper was crushed. Now what? \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1304Previous Page# / #1306 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Quelles qu'en soient les raisons, l'horrible règne de Caligula donna à "
"Rome le goût de ce qu'un gouvernement autocratique pouvait donner sous "
"un mauvais gouverneur. En janvier de l'an 41, les Romains en eurent "
"assez; une émeute menée par les Gardes Prétoriennes et plusieurs "
"sénateurs assassinèrent Caligula, puis sa femme et ses enfants. La "
"vipère était écrasée.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1304Page Précédente# / #1306 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

