msgctxt "help/1315/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory"
msgstr "Damné dans les mémoires"

msgctxt "help/1315/1"
msgid ""
"The single biggest mistake Claudius made during his reign was allowing "
"Agrippina to choose his successor for him. Agrippina's son, his name "
"changed to Nero Claudius Caesar Augustus Germanicus, was not yet "
"seventeen when his mother arranged his ascension to the Imperium. "
"Agrippina intended to rule through her son, but Nero's advisors Burrus "
"and Seneca brought the young Emperor under their control instead, "
"throwing Agrippina into a rage. In 55 AD Claudius's son Brittanicus was "
"poisoned; some historians believed Agrippina to be responsible, but "
"others say that Agrippina, furious over her son's rebellion, had begun "
"to conspire with Brittanicus to put him in Nero's place instead, "
"leading Nero to order Brittanicus killed. The following year, Agrippina "
"was forced into retirement to keep her from causing any more trouble.\n"
"\n"
"#1314Previous Page# / #1316 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Le seule grande erreur de Claudius a été de permettre à Agrippine de "
"choisir un successeur pour lui. Le fils d'Agrippine, dont le nom fut "
"changé en Néron Claudius Caesar Augustus Germanicus, n'avait pas encore "
"17 ans quand sa mère arrangea son accession au pouvoir. Agrippine "
"comptait diriger à travers son fils, mais les conseillers de Néron, "
"Burrus et Seneque, prirent l'Empereur sous leur contrôle, déclenchant "
"la rage d'Agrippine. En 55 ap. J.-C., le fils de Claudius Britannicus "
"fut empoisonné; certains historiens pensent que Agrippine était la "
"responsable, mais d'autres disent qu'Agrippine, furieuse de la "
"rébellion de son fils, commença à conspirer avec Britannicus pour "
"reprendre la place de Néron, forçant Néron à commander l'assassinat de "
"Britannicus. L'année suivante, on obligea Agrippine à prendre sa "
"retraite pour l'empêcher de causer plus de problème.\n"
"\n"
"#1314Page Précédente# / #1316 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1316/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr "Damné dans les mémoires (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1316/1"
msgid ""
"For the first five years of his reign, Nero showed himself to be a "
"remarkably kind and generous Emperor. His advisors encouraged him to "
"indulge his own personal pleasures while they did the real business of "
"running the Empire, which kept Nero happy. He passed laws forbidding "
"gladiatorial killings, and instituted poetry competitions and athletic "
"games in their place. He also reduced taxes and gave slaves the right "
"to complain against unjust owners. His generosity was both public and "
"private; in addition to sending aid to cities victimized by natural "
"disasters, he also pardoned writers of satirical verses, who would have "
"swiftly been executed by any earlier Emperor. In sum, Nero was an "
"irresponsible and naive but essentially harmless young man.\n"
"\n"
"This began to change in AD 59, when Nero was \n"
"#1315Previous Page# / #1317 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Pendant les cinq premières années de son règne, Néron se montra "
"remarquablement gentil et généreux. Ses conseillers l'encourageaient à "
"s'occuper de son propre plaisir, pour leur laisser mener les affaires "
"de l'Empire, ce qui ne pouvait que satisfaire le jeune Empereur. Il fit "
"voter des lois interdisant les tueries de gladiateurs, et institua un "
"concours de poésie et des jeux athlétiques. Il réduisit également les "
"taxes et permit aux esclaves de se plaindre contre les propriétaires "
"injustes. Sa générosité était à la fois publique et privée; tout en "
"envoyant de l'aide aux villes victimes des désastres naturels, il "
"pardonna les écrivains de vers satiriques qui auraient normalement été "
"exécutés par les Empereurs précédents. En résumé, Néron était un "
"irresponsable et un naïf mais restait un jeune homme inoffensif.\n"
"Le changement eut lieu en 59 ap. J.-C., quand Néron\n"
"#1315Page Précédente# / #1317 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1317/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr "Damné dans les mémoires (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1317/1"
msgid ""
"twenty one. His mother Agrippina had gone insane with rage and "
"threatened Nero; in his first proven act of official murder, Nero had "
"her put to death. Shortly after that, he came under the influence of a "
"woman named Poppaea Sabina, the young wife of a Senator, who became his "
"mistress. Poppaea supplanted Burrus and Seneca as Nero's chief advisor, "
"and she was not nearly so well suited for the task. Eventually she "
"convinced Nero to have his wife Octavia (who also happened to be "
"Claudius's daughter) murdered so that Nero could marry her instead, "
"which he did in 65 AD.\n"
"\n"
"After that point, it became apparent to Nero that he could do exactly "
"as he wished, and no one could gainsay him. What he wished was to be an "
"actor and a musician, not an Emperor, for he had proved to \n"
"#1316Previous Page# / #1318 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"atteignit l'âge de 21 ans. Sa mère Agrippine était devenue folle de "
"rage et le menaça; Néron la fit assassiner, son premier meurtre "
"officiel. Peu après, il tomba sous l'influence d'une femme nommée "
"Poppaea Sabina, la jeune épouse d'un sénateur, qui devint sa maîtresse. "
"Poppaea supplanta Burrus et Seneque à la place de conseiller chef de "
"Néron, et elle convainquit Néron de faire assassiner sa femme Octavia "
"(qui se trouvait être la fille de Claudius) pour se marier avec elle, "
"ce qu'il fit en 65 ap. J.-C.\n"
"\n"
"A ce moment-là, il devenait évident à Néron qu'il pouvait faire ce "
"qu'il désirait et que personne ne pourrait le contredire. Ce qu'il "
"voulait, c'était d'être acteur et musicien, pas Empereur; il ne montra "
"d'ailleurs\n"
"#1316Page Précédente# / #1318 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1318/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr "Damné dans les mémoires (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1318/1"
msgid ""
"have neither interest in nor talent for statesmanship. Nero began "
"acting on the stage, taking whatever roles he found interesting, and "
"also traveled all over the Empire participating in musical competitions "
"(which he always won, of course.) He also became involved in a number "
"of strange foreign cults. This behavior left the Senate and the morally "
"conservative Roman middle class aghast -- it was a serious affront to "
"the dignity and the decorum required of the ruler of a great Empire. "
"The army, too, was scandalized to see the descendent of their revered "
"Caesar behaving this way.\n"
"\n"
"In 64 AD a huge fire burned down much of Rome. Rumors whispered that "
"Nero himself had started it in order to be able to rebuild the city "
"more to his liking. The rumor wasn't true, for Nero was miles away at \n"
"#1317Previous Page# / #1319 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"aucun intérêt ni talent pour les affaires de l'état. Néron commença à "
"jouer dans des pièces, prenant les rôles qui l'intéressait, et voyagea "
"également dans tout l'Empire pour participer à des concours musicaux "
"(qu'il remportait chaque fois bien entendu) Il fut également impliqué "
"dans un certain nombre de cultes étrangers bizarres. Ce comportement "
"laissait le Sénat et la classe moyenne conservatrice atterrée -- "
"c'était un sérieux affront à la dignité et au décorum demandé à un "
"dirigeant du grand Empire. L'armée aussi, était scandalisée de voir "
"qu'un descendant du vénéré César agissait de cette façon.\n"
"\n"
"En 64 ap. J.-C., un énorme incendie ravagea Rome. Des rumeurs "
"prétendirent que Néron lui-même l'avait fait pour pouvoir reconstruire "
"la ville à son goût. La rumeur était fausse, car Néron se trouvait à "
"des kilomètres\n"
"#1317Page Précédente# / #1319 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1319/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr "Damné dans les mémoires (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1319/1"
msgid ""
"his villa during the fire (singing about the fall of Troy as he watched "
"the flames in the distance, according to legend). To deflect talk away "
"from himself, Nero blamed the fire on the new and unpopular Christian "
"religious sect, becoming the first Roman Emperor to persecute "
"Christians and thereby causing those unfortunates to regard him as the "
"Antichrist.\n"
"\n"
"When the fire died down, Nero ordered a huge (and hugely luxurious) new "
"home called the Golden Palace built for himself in the cleared area. "
"Had it been completed, it would have covered fully a third of Rome. To "
"pay for all this, Nero demanded higher and higher taxes from Rome and "
"the provinces, causing serious economic problems for the populace. "
"Combined with Nero's total lack of strong leadership, this oppression "
"led to severe unrest in parts of the \n"
"#1318Previous Page# / #1320 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"de là, dans sa villa pendant l'incendie (chantant sur la chute de Troie "
"tout en regardant les flammes à distance, selon la légende). Pour "
"détourner les conversations, Néron accusa une secte religieuse "
"chrétienne peu populaire, devenant ainsi le premier Empereur Romain à "
"persécuter les Chrétiens et acquérant ainsi la réputation d'Antéchrist.\n"
"\n"
"Quand l'incendie fut éteint, Néron ordonna la construction d'une "
"nouvelle demeure, gigantesque, le Palais d'Or. A la fin des travaux, "
"elle couvrirait une surface équivalent à un tiers de Rome. Pour la "
"financer, Néron réclama des taxes de plus en plus élevées à Rome et aux "
"provinces, créant de sérieux problèmes économiques dans la population. "
"Avec un manque total du sens du commandement, cette oppression engendra "
"de sérieuses émeutes\n"
"#1318Page Précédente# / #1320 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1320/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr "Damné dans les mémoires (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1320/1"
msgid ""
"Empire; Britain rose in revolt, as did Judaea and Gaul, and the "
"Parthians pushed into Roman lands in the east.\n"
"\n"
"By 65 AD, conspiracies to get rid of Nero had begun to form. The "
"Emperor was protected for a time by warnings from slaves of the "
"conspirators, who still remembered the rights he had given them. They "
"could not guard him forever, though. Unable to dispose of Nero by "
"subtler means, the Romans rose in open revolt in 68 AD. Legions in Gaul "
"and Spain proclaimed their commanders Emperor. Even the Praetorian "
"Guard repudiated Nero. The Senate, in an emergency meeting, declared "
"that Nero's life was forfeit, and decreed that he should be flogged to "
"death on the cross. Nero fled from Rome, then ended the Julio-Claudian "
"line of Emperors ignobly by \n"
"#1319Previous Page# / #1321 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"dans l'Empire; la Bretagne se révolta, ainsi que la Judée et la Gaule, "
"et les Parthes envahirent les terres romaines à l'est.\n"
"En 65 ap. J.-C., les conspirations contre Néron commencèrent à se "
"former. L'Empereur était protégé pendant un temps grâce aux "
"avertissements de la part des esclaves des conspirateurs, qui se "
"souvenaient toujours des droits qu'ils leur avait conférés. Mais cela "
"ne dura pas. Incapable de disposer de moyens plus subtiles, les Romains "
"se révoltèrent en 68 ap. J.-C. Les légions en Gaule et en Espagne "
"proclamèrent leur commandant Empereur. Même la Garde Prétorienne "
"répudiait Néron. Le Sénat, lors d'une réunion extraordinaire, déclara "
"que la vie de Néron était déchue, et décréta qu'il devrait être "
"flagellé à mort suspendu sur un croix. Néron s'enfuit de Rome et mit "
"fin à la lignée des Empereurs Julio-Claudiens en \n"
"#1319Page Précédente# / #1321 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1321/0"
msgid "Damned in Memory (Cont.)"
msgstr "Damné dans les mémoires (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1321/1"
msgid ""
"cutting his own throat. The Senate, far from declaring Nero a god, "
"decreed him \"Damnatio Memoriae\" -- damned in memory -- and had all "
"mention of his reign stricken from the records. \n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1320Previous Page# / #1325 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"se tranchant la gorge. Le Sénat, loin de déifier Néron, le déclara "
"\"Damnatio Memoriae\"-- damné dans les mémoires -- et effaça toute trace "
"de son règne dans les écrits.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
