msgctxt "help/1335/0"
msgid "The Sons of Vespasian"
msgstr "Les fils de Vespasien"

msgctxt "help/1335/1"
msgid ""
"One of Vespasian's most notable achievements was that he convinced the "
"Roman armies to accept a line other than that of Julius Caesar as their "
"rulers. Vespasian was determined to found a dynasty, saying \"Either my "
"sons will succeed me, or no one will.\" As it happened, both of them "
"did.\n"
"\n"
"Vespasian's eldest son and heir, Titus, was one of Rome's most beloved "
"and most unfortunate Emperors. His father carefully groomed him as his "
"heir, and had all but shared the Principate with him in the last few "
"years before his death. But Titus died of a fever only two years after "
"his ascension, during which time Vesuvius erupted, destroying the "
"cities of Pompeii and Herculaneum, and a huge fire swept through Rome. "
"If he had lived longer, he might have had time to make enemies; "
"instead, he died with the first \n"
"#1334Previous Page# / #1336 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Un des accomplissements les plus notables de Vespasien fut de "
"convaincre l'armée romaine d'accepter une autre lignée que celle de "
"Julius Caesar, à leur tête. Vespasien était déterminé à fonder une "
"dynastie, disant \"Mes fils me succéderont ou personne ne le fera\". En "
"fait, ses deux fils lui succédèrent.\n"
"L'aîné des fils de Vespasien, et son héritier, Titus, fut l'un des "
"Empereurs les plus adorés et les plus malchanceux de l'histoire de "
"Rome. Son père le forma soigneusement pour en faire son héritier, et "
"partagea même le Principat avec lui pendant ses dernières années. Mais "
"Titus mourut d'une fièvre à peine deux ans après son accession, à "
"l'époque de l'éruption du Vésuve qui détruisit les villes de Pompéï et "
"Herculanum, et d'un gigantesque incendie à Rome. S'il avait vécu plus "
"longtemps, il aurait pu se faire des ennemis; mais il mourut au tout\n"
"#1334Page Précédente# / #1336 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1336/0"
msgid "The Sons of Vespasian (Cont.)"
msgstr "Les fils de Vespasien (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1336/1"
msgid ""
"bloom still on his reign, and was instantly deified.\n"
"Vespasian had never bothered to adequately prepare his second son, "
"Domitian, for the job of Emperor. In fact, he and Titus both appear to "
"have gone to lengths to keep Domitian from acquiring any power or "
"influence in Rome while they were alive. Possibly Vespasian simply "
"favored Titus so strongly that he wanted no competition for his eldest "
"son's rule, or perhaps he was aware that his youngest son was not "
"Emperor material. For whatever reason, Domitian's father had never "
"considered him a successor -- so naturally Domitian became fiercely "
"jealous of his own power when he gained the Imperial seat.\n"
"Domitian ruled as an autocrat from the very first. He \n"
"\n"
"#1335Previous Page# / #1337 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"début de son règne, et fut instantanément déifié.\n"
"Vespasien ne s'était jamais préoccupé de préparer son second fils, "
"Domitien, à la fonction d'Empereur. En fait, Titus et lui semblent "
"toujours avoir voulu écarté Domitien du pouvoir et de l'influence de "
"Rome. Il est possible que Vespasien préférait tout simplement Titus "
"qu'il ne supportait aucune compétition pour le règne de son fils aîné, "
"ou pensait-il que son fils cadet n'était pas fait pour être Empereur. "
"Quelles qu'en soient les raisons, le père de Domitien ne l'avait jamais "
"considéré comme son successeur -- Domitien devint naturellement "
"horriblement jaloux de son propre pouvoir quand il acquit le siège "
"impérial.\n"
"Domitien régnait de façon autocratique dès le début. Il\n"
"\n"
"#1335Page Précédente# / #1337 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1337/0"
msgid "The Sons of Vespasian (Cont.)"
msgstr "Les fils de Vespasien (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1337/1"
msgid ""
"scorned the Senate, ignoring them most of the time and humiliating them "
"when he did deign to notice their existence. The legions, by contrast, "
"he pampered and showered with favors. Their support no doubt allowed "
"him to stay in power far longer than he would have otherwise.\n"
"\n"
" In 86 AD, five years into his reign, he decided not to await the "
"posthumous decision of the Senate and declared himself a living god. "
"Grumbling from the Senate and the citizens of Rome was met with a "
"flurry of treason trials and executions, which continued and grew more "
"common and baseless until Domitian held the entire Roman upper class in "
"a reign of terror. Publicly, Domitian decreed that Romans should be "
"held to a high standard of morality, with lapses punished severely. "
"This was \n"
"#1336Previous Page# / #1338 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"dédaigna le Sénat, l'ignorant la plupart du temps, et l'humiliant quand "
"il daignait remarquer son existence. Les légions, au contraire, "
"recueillaient ses faveurs et son suffrage. C'est sans doute leur "
"soutien qui lui a permis  de rester aussi longtemps au pourvoir.\n"
"\n"
"En 86 ap. J.-C., cinq ans après son ascension, il décida de ne pas "
"attendre la décision posthume du Sénat et se proclama lui-même dieu "
"vivant. En persécutant le Sénat et les citoyens de Rome de procès et "
"d'exécutions pour trahison, Domitien avait fini par tenir toute la "
"classe supérieure dans un règne de terreur. Publiquement, Domitien "
"décréta que les Romains devaient respecter un code moral strict et "
"élevé, et que tout manquement serait sévèrement punis. Cela donnait\n"
"#1336Page Précédente# / #1338 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1338/0"
msgid "The Sons of Vespasian (Cont.)"
msgstr "Les fils de Vespasien (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1338/1"
msgid ""
"little more than another excuse for Domitian to exercise his supreme "
"power, though; in his private life, he did as he pleased, even going so "
"far as to take one of his own nieces as a mistress.\n"
"\n"
"Yet Domitian's tyranny was mostly restricted to the city of Rome "
"itself. He selected competent governors for the provinces, who were too "
"scared of Domitian to risk misbehaving. Militarily, Domitian showed "
"little interest in acquiring new lands for Rome; his activity in this "
"sphere was restrained to putting down a rebellion in Africa, expanding "
"the province of Britain into Scotland, and struggling with recurring "
"problems in Dacia. At home, Domitian's sole positive accomplishment was "
"to promote a good deal of solid and worthwhile construction in Rome. He "
"had no financial talent, though, and caused economic \n"
"#1337Previous Page# / #1339 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"une excuse supplémentaire à Domitien pour exercer son pouvoir suprême; "
"dans sa vie privée, il faisait comme il entendait, allant même jusqu'à "
"prendre pour maîtresse l'un des ses nièces.\n"
"Mais la tyrannie de Domitien concernait strictement la cille de Rome "
"même. Il choisissait des gouverneurs compétents pour les provinces, qui "
"avaient trop peu de Domitien pour risquer de commettre des mauvais pas. "
"Sur le plan militaire, Domitien montra peu d'intérêt en acquérant de "
"nouvelles terres pour Rome; son activité en cette matière s'est limitée "
"à contenir une rébellion en Afrique, étendre la province de Bretagne "
"vers l'Ecosse, et luttant avec les problèmes récurrents à Dacia. Chez "
"lui, le seul accomplissement positif de Domitien fut de promouvoir un "
"plan de reconstruction et d'embellissement de Rome. Il n'avait "
"cependant aucun talent de gestionnaire et déclencha des problèmes "
"économiques \n"
"#1337Page Précédente# / #1339 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1339/0"
msgid "The Sons of Vespasian (Cont.)"
msgstr "Les fils de Vespasien (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1339/1"
msgid ""
"problems by overspending on his building projects.\n"
"\n"
"The last few years of the Principate of Domitian were filled with "
"nonstop treason trials and executions that seriously depleted the "
"Senate body. Finally, the Praetorian Guard captain (with the help of "
"Domitian's wife) arranged to have the tyrant assassinated. The Senate "
"-- or what was left of it -- fell over itself in its haste to declare "
"Domitian's reign Damnatio Memoriae.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1338Previous Page# / #1345 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"en finançant ses projets de construction.\n"
"\n"
"Les dernières années du Principat de Domitien étaient remplies "
"d'incessants procès de trahisons et d'exécutions qui décimèrent "
"sérieusement le corps du sénat. Finalement, le capitaine de la Garde "
"Prétorienne (avec l'aide de la femme de Domitien), s'arrangea pour "
"assassiner le tyran. Le Sénat -- du moins ce qu'il en restait -- avoua "
"sa propre haine en déclarant le règne de Domitien Damnatio Memoriae.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1338Page Précédente# / #1345 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

