msgctxt "help/1354/0"
msgid "Frontier Guardian"
msgstr "Gardien des frontières"

msgctxt "help/1354/1"
msgid ""
"While preparing for a campaign in Mesopotamia, Trajan fell ill of a "
"fever. On his deathbed, he adopted Publius Aelius Hadrianus -- a "
"distant relative and a longtime companion -- as his son and named him "
"heir. Like his predecessor, Hadrian was a Spaniard and a general, but "
"unlike Trajan he returned to Augustus's policy of containing the growth "
"of the Empire within defensible boundaries. One of his first actions as "
"Princeps was to pull back from Trajan's conquests in the East, "
"abandoning Assyria and returning Armenia's status to that of client "
"kingdom instead of province. His retreat from Assyria caused grumbling "
"among some of the other Roman generals, but was undoubtedly a wise "
"move; the new province had not truly been conquered, merely overrun, "
"and was already gearing up for rebellion. In England and Germany he "
"built walls to help hold back barbarian \n"
"#1353Previous Page# / #1355 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"Tandis qu'il préparait une nouvelle campagne militaire en Mésopotamie, "
"Trajan tomba très malade et fut pris de violentes fièvres. Sur son lit "
"de mort, il adopta Publius Aelius Hadrianus - un membre lointain de sa "
"famille, ami de longue date - pour fils et en fit son héritier. S'il "
"était comme son prédécesseur général et Espagnol, ne partageait "
"cependant pas ses convictions concernant l'expansion de l'Empire, et "
"revint aux théories d'Auguste selon lesquelles il fallait le conserver "
"dans des frontières défendables. L'un de ses premiers geste fut de se "
"retirer des conquêtes de l'est, abandonnant l'Assyrie et restaurant "
"pour l'Arménie le statut d'état allié de Rome, et non de province. Si "
"sa retraite d'Assyrie causa le mécontentement de bien des généraux "
"Romains, elle n'en était pas moins une décision des plus sages; cette "
"nouvelle province n'avait en effet jamais totalement +\n"
"#1353Page Précédente# / #1355 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1355/0"
msgid "Frontier Guardian (Cont.)"
msgstr "Gardien des frontières (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1355/1"
msgid ""
"encroachments.\n"
"\n"
"Hadrian was a versatile and liberal Emperor, equally capable in the "
"military, administrative, and political aspects of his position. He was "
"strongly influenced by Greek thought, and believed that \"The ruler "
"exists for the state, not the state for the ruler.\" His provincial "
"origins led him to view Italy and Rome as a part of a greater whole, "
"and he promoted many provincials into Senate seats. Hadrian's most "
"serious administrative change came when his removed the requirement "
"that equites must serve in the military before they could have a career "
"with the civil service; although this worked well for a while, it began "
"a separation between the military and civil arms of the government that "
"would have major consequences in later years.\n"
"\n"
"#1354Previous Page# / #1356 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"été conquise, juste envahie, et la rébellion se préparait déjà. Par "
"ailleurs, il fit en Angleterre et en Allemagne construire d'imposantes "
"murailles pour se prémunir des invasions de barbares.\n"
"#1354Page Précédente# / #1356 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1356/0"
msgid "Frontier Guardian (Cont.)"
msgstr "Gardien des frontières (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1356/1"
msgid ""
"Upon his ascension, Hadrian renewed the oath of Nerva and Trajan to "
"refrain from executing Senators, and showed respect to the Senate. Yet "
"the Senate never fully liked or trusted him. He lacked Trajan's "
"personal charisma; his foreign policy seemed weak and lacking in glory; "
"and he paid too much attention to the provinces and not enough to Rome "
"to suit them. And yet he reigned for over twenty years with no serious "
"unrest anywhere in the Empire.\n"
"\n"
"In 115 AD, Hadrian was struck with a debilitating progressive illness. "
"At that point his rule became rather tyrannical, and he began a frantic "
"search for a suitable successor. He had no children or close allies to "
"choose from; his personality had always been rather aloof, which was "
"part of the reason the Senate never warmed to him. After discarding "
"several possibilities, \n"
"#1355Previous Page# / #1357 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"A l'instar de Nerva et de Trajan, Hadrien fit lui aussi le serment de "
"ne pas exercer de répression féroce envers les membres du Sénat, et "
"respecta tout autant cette institution. Pourtant, le Sénat ne lui "
"rendit jamais tout à fait l'affection et la confiance qu'il plaçait en "
"lui. Il lui manquait le charisme d'un Trajan, sa politique étrangère "
"semblait aux tribuns frileuse et bien peu glorieuse, et il accordait "
"trop d'attention aux provinces au détriment de Rome. En 115 ap. J.-C., "
"Hadrien fut frappé par une maladie débilitante incurable. Son règne "
"devint alors bien plus tyrannique qu'auparavant, et il se mit à se "
"chercher un successeur avec ferveur. Il n'avait aucun enfant ou allié "
"proche pouvant remplir cet office; il avait toujours eu une attitude "
"distante, ce qui est du reste l'une des raisons pour lesquelles il "
"n'avait jamais été apprécié du Sénat. Après avoir considéré puis écarté "
"diverses solutions,\n"
"#1355Page Précédente# / #1357 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1357/0"
msgid "Frontier Guardian (Cont.)"
msgstr "Gardien des frontières (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1357/1"
msgid ""
"he settled on Titus Aurelius Antoninus, a Senator already over fifty "
"years old, and adopted him as son and heir. Apparently worried that his "
"first choice was nearing the end of his life, he made the Senator adopt "
"two boys of Hadrian's choosing to be heirs after Antoninus: sixteen "
"year old Marcus Aurelius Antoninus, and seven year old Lucius Verus. "
"Hadrian passed away soon after.\n"
"\n"
"TRANQUILLATAS AND CONCORDIA\n"
"\n"
"Hadrian needn't have worried about Titus Marcus Antoninus's health. The "
"new Princeps reigned for over twenty years, and according to some "
"sources became Rome's \"most beloved Emperor of all.\" The coins minted "
"in his day bore the slogans \"Tranquillitatas\" and \"Concordia\" -- "
"Tranquillity and \n"
"#1356Previous Page# / #1358 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"il fixa son choix sur Titus Aurelius Antoninus, un sénateur âgé de plus "
"de cinquante ans, et l'adopta afin d'en faire son héritier. Mais "
"inquiet de ce que son poulain soit lui-même proche de la mort, il lui "
"fit à son tour adopter deux jeunes garçons de son choix, Marcus "
"Aurelius Antoninus (Marc-Aurèle), seize ans, et Lucius Verus, de neuf "
"ans son cadet. Peu après ces événements, Hadrien passa de vie à trépas.\n"
"TRANQUILLATAS ET CONCORDIA\n"
"Hadrien n'avait aucune raison de se faire du souçi pour la santé de son "
"héritier, puisque le nouveau César régna lui aussi durant plus de vingt "
"ans, et fut selon quelques sources\"l'Empereur le plus aimé de tous\". "
"Les pièces frappées sous Antoine portaient gravés les mots "
"\"Tranquillitatas\"et\"Concordia\", littéralement\n"
"#1356Page Précédente# / #1358 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1358/0"
msgid "Tranquillatas and Concordia (Cont.)"
msgstr "Tranquillatas et Concordia (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1358/1"
msgid ""
"Peace.\n"
"\n"
"Antoninus's first act was to pressure the Senate to deify Hadrian. The "
"Senate was reluctant -- Hadrian had not been all that popular in Rome, "
"despite his competence -- but Antoninus's impassioned speeches both "
"persuaded the Senate to make Hadrian a god of the state and also to "
"bestow the nickname \"Pius\" on Antoninus, for his devotion to his "
"adoptive father. Antoninus, like Hadrian and Trajan, was a provincial. "
"His family hailed from Gallia Narbonensis, and his wife was a Spaniard.\n"
"\n"
"Records from Antoninus's reign are scarce, but what little is available "
"is uniformly positive. The new Princeps managed to pull off a trick "
"many modern politicians would like to duplicate: he lowered taxes \n"
"#1357Previous Page# / #1359 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"\"tranquillité\"et\"paix\".\n"
"La première action entreprise au début de son règne fut d'exercer une "
"pression considérable sur le sénat afin de le contraindre à déifier "
"Hadrien. Comme nous l'avons vu plus haut, ce dernier n'a en effet "
"jamais été très populaire, en dépit de ses compétences, mais les "
"discours enflammés d'Antoine finirent par avoir raison des scrupules du "
"Sénat, qui précéda également le nom d'Antoine du préfixe\"Pius\", en "
"hommage à son père adoptif. A l'instar des deux précédents césars, "
"Antoine était un provincial, né de père Gaulois et de mère Espagnole.\n"
"Les témoignages du règne d'Antoine sont hélas aujourd'hui très rares, "
"mais le peu qu'il nous reste est unanimement positif. Le nouveau "
"Princeps parvint à réaliser un tour de force dont rêvent bien des "
"politiciens contemporains : celui de baisser les impôts\n"
"#1357Page Précédente# / #1359 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

msgctxt "help/1359/0"
msgid "Tranquillatas and Concordia (Cont.)"
msgstr "Tranquillatas et Concordia (Suite)"

msgctxt "help/1359/1"
msgid ""
"while at the same time increasing public spending. He reformed parts of "
"Rome's judicial code to promote impartiality and equity under the law. "
"He defended and maintained Rome's borders but started no wars. The "
"barbarian tribes made a few raids into Roman territory, which boded ill "
"for the future, yet presented Antoninus no serious problems.\n"
"\n"
"To secure the succession, he honored Hadrian's wishes and married his "
"daughter to Marcus Aurelius, one of the boys Hadrian had ordered him to "
"adopt, and named him heir. On his death in 161 AD, he was widely and "
"genuinely mourned, and promptly deified. As a further tribute, the "
"entire line of good emperors of Rome's Golden Age was given his name: "
"the Antonines.\n"
"\n"
"\n"
"#1358Previous Page# / #1360 Next Page#\n"
"#3Back to Table of Contents#"
msgstr ""
"tout en augmentant les dépenses publiques. Il mit également en oeuvre "
"une réforme du code civil romain visant à rétablir l'impartialité de la "
"justice et l'équité des citoyens devant la loi. Enfin, s'il dut bien "
"défendre en quelques occasions des frontières de l'Empire menacées, et "
"repousser quelques raids de barbares, il eut le mérite de ne déclarer "
"aucune guerre durant son règne.\n"
"Afin d'assurer sa succession, Antoine accéda aux souhaits passés "
"d'Hadrien et maria sa fille à Marc-Aurèle, l'un des enfants qui lui "
"avaient été ordonné d'adopter, et en fit son héritier. A sa mort, en "
"161 ap. J.-C., il fut longtemps pleuré et regretté dans tout l'Empire, "
"et promptement déifié. Enfin, en guise d'ultime hommage, toute la "
"lignée des Empereurs de l'Age d'Or furent dotée de son nom : les "
"Antonins.\n"
"\n"
"#1358Page Précédente# / #1360 Page Suivante#\n"
"#3Retour au sommaire#"

